window.QIQI_SEARCH = [{"title":"Getting started: your first session","text":"The start menu, creating a world, moving, reading the screen, opening your pack, switching modes, and saving safely.","href":"guide-getting-started.html","kind":"Guide"},{"title":"Controls reference","text":"The essential keys, the keys that change meaning with context, and a searchable list of every control in the audited game.","href":"guide-controls.html","kind":"Guide"},{"title":"Your first survival day","text":"Switch to survival, read the four bars, drink, eat, mine, make stone tools, cook, build a home and avoid the real dangers.","href":"guide-first-survival-day.html","kind":"Guide"},{"title":"Crafting and recipes","text":"How the Craft tab works, which ingredients can be mixed, the quick-craft trap, and the complete recipe list with amounts.","href":"guide-crafting.html","kind":"Guide"},{"title":"Install, update and find your worlds","text":"Install Voxelgame on Linux, Windows or macOS, update it by running the installer again, and find the folders that hold your worlds.","href":"guide-install-and-worlds.html","kind":"Guide"},{"title":"Creative and survival modes (F4)","text":"There are exactly two modes. Press F4 any time to switch between creative and survival; your pack, hotbar and position stay as they are. New worlds always start in creative, and each world remembers the mode it was saved in. F4: Switch creative / survival (in the world (refused while connected to a server))","href":"features.html#f-game-modes","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Start menu","text":"The start menu offers Continue and Worlds for opening worlds, plus Settings, Credits, Study timer and Quit, with the current world's name shown above the buttons. Use the mouse or the Up/Down arrows and Enter; Esc returns from any sub-page. Up / Down arrows: Move the selection (start menu) Enter or click: Open the selected entry (start menu) Esc: Return to the first page (start-menu sub-page) P: Open Study Grove (start menu, not typing on the Worlds page)","href":"features.html#f-start-menu","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Worlds: create, load, search and delete","text":"Worlds lists your saves, current world first, then alphabetically. Type to filter the list or to name a new world, then choose Create world. Click a row or press Enter to load it. To delete a world, hold its DELETE badge or the Delete key for 3 seconds; the current world cannot be deleted. Typing: Filter the list / name the next new world (Worlds page) Backspace / Ctrl+Backspace: Delete one character / clear the field (Worlds page) Up / Down arrows: Move through worlds, Create world and Back (Worlds page) Enter or click a row: Load that world (Worlds page) Enter or click Create world: Create and enter a new world (Worlds page) Hold Delete (row selected) or hold the DELETE badge: Delete that world after 3 s (Worlds page, not the current world) Esc or Back: Return to the start menu (Worlds page)","href":"features.html#f-worlds-page","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Settings menu basics","text":"Press Esc in the world to open Settings; the game pauses and the panel is titled 'Paused'. Switch between the General, Audio, World and Debug tabs with the mouse or Tab/Shift+Tab, pick chips or drag sliders, or use the arrow keys. Resume, Main menu and Save & quit are at the bottom. Esc: Open Settings / close it again (in the world) Tab / Shift+Tab: Next / previous tab (Settings) Up / Down arrows: Select a row (Settings) Left / Right arrows (Enter = Right): Change the selected value (Settings) Click a chip / drag a slider: Set a value directly (Settings) Right click a row: Step that value backward (Settings) Resume / Main menu / Save & quit: Return to play / save and go to the start menu / save and exit (in-game Settings) Back: Return to the start menu (start-menu Settings)","href":"features.html#f-settings-menu","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Esc key and what pauses the game","text":"Esc closes whatever is open (pack panel, large map, Study Grove, a start-menu sub-page) and recaptures the mouse; with nothing open it opens Settings and frees the mouse. Settings, the large map, the start menu and Study Grove pause the world; the pack panel does not. Esc: Close the open layer, or open Settings (anywhere)","href":"features.html#f-escape-and-pause","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Mouse capture and release","text":"Click the world to capture the mouse; that click never mines or places. Opening a panel, Esc, Print Screen, losing focus or being covered releases it, and it is not recaptured when focus returns. While released you cannot move or look. Click the world: Capture the mouse (click is not used in game) (pointer released, no panel open) Print Screen: Release the mouse for a system screenshot tool (anywhere) Esc: Open Settings and release the mouse (in the world)","href":"features.html#f-mouse-capture","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Saving, Main menu and quitting","text":"The world autosaves about every 30 seconds when it has unsaved changes, and F6 saves at once ('World saved safely'). Settings > Main menu saves and returns to the start menu. Ctrl+Q, Save & quit, Quit or closing the window all save first; if saving fails, the game stays open. F6: Save the world now (anywhere in a session) Ctrl+Q: Save and quit (anywhere in a session) Settings > Save & quit: Save and quit (in-game Settings) Settings > Main menu: Save and return to the start menu (in-game Settings) Start menu > Quit / window close button: Save and quit (start menu / anywhere)","href":"features.html#f-saving-and-quitting","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Window, fullscreen and title","text":"F11 or Alt+Enter toggles borderless fullscreen. The window opens at 1280x720 and cannot shrink below 800x450. Its title shows loading stages, 'Main Menu', 'Paused / Settings', the latest status message, or a detailed FPS/position line while FPS and coordinates is on. Launching again just focuses the open game. F11 or Alt+Enter: Toggle borderless fullscreen (anywhere)","href":"features.html#f-window-and-fullscreen","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Keyboard, mouse and controller support","text":"The game is played with keyboard and mouse only: there is no gamepad support and keys cannot be rebound. Keys are read by physical position, so on non-QWERTY keyboards use the keys where W A S D sit on a QWERTY board.","href":"features.html#f-input-devices-and-layout","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"New worlds: creative by default, survival starter supplies","text":"New worlds open in creative mode; press F4 to switch to survival. Every new world's pack already holds 12 Berries, 2 Water flasks, 1 Empty flask and 4 Alder board pieces. F4: Switch between creative and survival (in a world; refused while connected to a server)","href":"features.html#f-new-world-start","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Credits","text":"Choose Credits on the main menu to watch seven title cards with authorship, acknowledgements and a personal dedication on a black stage with drifting golden particles. Each card shows for 12 seconds and the sequence loops; choose Skip credits or press Esc, Enter or Space to return. Esc / Enter / Space / Skip credits: Leave the credits (Credits screen)","href":"features.html#f-credits","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Walking and looking around","text":"Move with W A S D and look with the mouse once the pointer is captured. You walk at about 4.3 m/s, climb half-metre steps without jumping, and need to jump onto a full one-metre block. W / A / S / D: Walk forward / left / back / right (on foot, pointer captured) Mouse: Look around (pointer captured, no panel open)","href":"features.html#f-walk-and-look","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Jumping","text":"Press Space to jump about 1.25 m high; keep Space held to jump again every time you land. While crawling, Space asks to stand up instead of jumping. Space: Jump (on the ground) Space (hold): Jump again on each landing (on the ground)","href":"features.html#f-jump","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Sprinting","text":"Hold Ctrl while moving forward with W to sprint at about 5.6 m/s. A sprint-jump adds a small forward push. In survival, sprinting uses stamina and stops when stamina is nearly empty. Ctrl + W (hold): Sprint (on foot, not sneaking or crawling) Ctrl + Space while sprinting: Sprint-jump with a small forward push (on foot)","href":"features.html#f-sprint","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Sneaking (ledge guard)","text":"Hold Shift on the ground to sneak at about 1.3 m/s with a slightly lower view. While sneaking you cannot walk off an edge, and the hint under the crosshair reads 'SNEAK: LEDGE GUARD ACTIVE'. Shift (hold): Sneak with ledge protection (on the ground, not flying or in water)","href":"features.html#f-sneak-ledge-guard","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Crawling","text":"Press C on foot to crawl with a 0.6 m tall body at about 1 m/s to get through low gaps. Press C again or Space to stand; you only stand once there is full headroom, and in a low space the game keeps you crawling automatically. C: Start or stop crawling (on foot, not flying or swimming) Space: Ask to stand up (while crawling)","href":"features.html#f-crawl","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Swimming and diving","text":"Once water reaches your chest you swim. W and S follow the direction you look (look down to dive), Space swims up, Shift dives and Ctrl swims faster; with no keys you hold your depth. Look + W / S: Swim toward / away from where you look (swimming) Space (hold): Swim upward (any part of the body in water) Shift (hold): Dive (in water) Ctrl + W (hold): Swim faster (swimming)","href":"features.html#f-swimming-diving","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Creative flight","text":"In creative, press F or double-tap Space on dry land to start or stop flying. Hold Space to rise, Shift to descend and Ctrl to boost from 16 to 32 m/s. Flight also ends when you switch to survival, board a vehicle or respawn. F: Toggle flight (creative) Space, Space (double-tap): Toggle flight (creative, on dry land, not in water, panel closed) Space (hold): Rise (flying) Shift (hold): Descend (flying) Ctrl (hold): Fly at boosted speed (flying) W / A / S / D: Fly horizontally (flying)","href":"features.html#f-creative-flight","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Hold to zoom","text":"Hold Z to smoothly narrow your view to 30 degrees for a closer look. A 'ZOOM' label appears above the crosshair and mouse look slows while zoomed. Z (hold): Zoom in (on foot, pointer captured, pack closed, Ctrl not held)","href":"features.html#f-hold-to-zoom","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Camera perspectives (F5)","text":"Press F5 to cycle First person, Third person: rear and Third person: front. Third-person cameras sit up to 4 m away and pull in before walls; the rear view keeps a crosshair where your aim lands, the front view hides it. F5: Cycle first person / rear / front view (on foot, pointer captured, pack closed) F5 or C: Cycle the boat's camera stations (inside a boat)","href":"features.html#f-camera-perspectives","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Camera bob and speed field of view","text":"Walking gently bobs the camera; turn it off in Settings > General > Camera bob. Moving fast widens the view by up to 5 degrees beyond your field-of-view setting, and there is no option to turn that widening off.","href":"features.html#f-camera-bob-and-speed-fov","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"HUD overview","text":"During play the status line sits top-left, the looking-at card top-centre, the compact map top-right with the FPS/coordinates box beneath it, the crosshair and action hint in the middle, the four survival bars lower-left (survival only) and the ten-slot hotbar with its item label at the bottom.","href":"features.html#f-hud-layout","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Crosshair and aiming reticle","text":"A small plus-shaped crosshair marks your aim and turns green-tinted when a block is in reach. With a bow, rifle or throwable it becomes a ring of dots whose size shows your current inaccuracy, shifting from orange to green as your aim steadies.","href":"features.html#f-crosshair-and-reticle","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Looking-at card (F2)","text":"When you aim at a block within 6 m, a card at the top-centre shows its 3D icon, name, distance and mining advice ('CREATIVE INSTANT BREAK', a recommended tool, or 'NO SPECIAL TOOL NEEDED'). Animals get their own card with health. Press F2 to hide or show it. F2: Hide / show the looking-at card (in the world)","href":"features.html#f-looking-at-card","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Clean view (F1)","text":"Press F1 to hide the HUD, crosshair, prompts, compact map, block highlight and first-person hands and tools, for example for screenshots. Menus still appear when opened. Press F1 again to bring everything back. F1: Hide / restore the HUD (in the world)","href":"features.html#f-clean-view","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"FPS and coordinates readout","text":"A small box under the compact map shows FPS (with the frame limit), your X, Y and Z position and the biome name. It is on by default; turn it off in Settings > Debug > FPS and coordinates (there is no hotkey).","href":"features.html#f-fps-coordinates-readout","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Status line, pickup and damage feedback","text":"Actions report in the top-left status line, for example 'Collected \u003cblock> x\u003cpieces>', 'Capacity full; block left behind' or 'Inventory opened'. Mined blocks throw debris and play a break sound, and pieces go straight into your pack. Taking damage only lowers the HP bar: there is no hurt sound, red flash or camera bob.","href":"features.html#f-feedback-messages","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Moving and climbing through leaves","text":"Tree leaves are solid: you bump into foliage and cannot walk or climb through it. Passing and scaffold-style climbing through leaves is requested but not implemented.","href":"features.html#f-move-through-leaves","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Health, hunger, thirst and stamina","text":"Survival shows four bars: HP, HUNGER, THIRST and STAMINA (0-100). A full hunger bar lasts about 40 minutes and a full thirst bar about 30 minutes; health slowly regenerates while both stay above 65. Ctrl + W: Sprint (uses stamina; needs more than 3%) (on foot or swimming)","href":"features.html#f-survival-meters","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Eating (R)","text":"Press R to eat one food when you are not full: Berries first, then Venison, Raw meat, and Cooked meat last. Click a food in the Pack to eat that one instead. R: Eat the first available food (survival, on foot) Click a food in Pack: Eat that food (inventory open)","href":"features.html#f-eating","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Drinking and water flasks (T)","text":"Press T next to drinkable water to drink (+22 thirst) and refill every Empty flask you carry. Away from water, T drinks one Water flask (+35) and you keep the Empty flask. T: Drink at water and fill all empty flasks, or drink a carried flask (survival) Click Water flask in Pack: Same as T (inventory open)","href":"features.html#f-drinking-flasks","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Body temperature and shelter","text":"Your body temperature drifts toward the air temperature and costs health only outside -12 to 48 C. Air temperature depends on time of day, season, rain and altitude (not biome); a roof and walls pull it toward 20 C.","href":"features.html#f-temperature-shelter","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Fall damage and other dangers","text":"Falls of up to 3 m are free; each extra metre costs 5 HP, so about 23 m is fatal from full health. Animals never attack; besides falls, only starvation, thirst, extreme cold and explosions hurt you.","href":"features.html#f-fall-damage-hazards","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Death and respawn","text":"At 0 HP you respawn immediately at your rest point (the world start point until you rest at home) and lose nothing: pack, hotbar and home storage stay as they were.","href":"features.html#f-death-respawn","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Home crate: establish, storage, repair, pack up","text":"In the Home tab, Establish home while standing on dry, solid ground: it costs 8 board pieces (free in creative) and places a small crate 1.5 m beside you. The crate stores up to 400 kg while you are within 6 m. Click Establish home: Place the crate (or repair a lost one at its original spot) (Home tab) Click a carried / stored row: Move one item into / out of storage (Home tab, within 6 m of the crate) Shift+click a row: Move as many as fit (Home tab) Click Pack up: Remove the crate; survival refunds 8 Alder boards (Home tab, storage empty)","href":"features.html#f-home-crate","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Resting and setting your respawn point","text":"Home tab > Rest / set respawn works within 6 m of your crate, on dry ground, under a roof with cover on at least two sides. It sets your respawn point there and refills stamina, but does not heal or feed you.","href":"features.html#f-rest-respawn-point","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Home tab trip-planning readout","text":"The bottom of the Home tab estimates how many minutes of food and water you carry, counts empty flasks, shows free pack space, whether your spot is sheltered, and the air temperature.","href":"features.html#f-trip-preparation-readout","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Achievements and survival progression goals","text":"Achievements/milestones and a reviewed survival progression are planned; the only progress tracking today is the Journal's three landmark discoveries and their one-time finds.","href":"features.html#f-planned-achievements-progression","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Planting, growth, fruit trees and berry bushes","text":"Plantable saplings/seeds, growth, harvestable fruit trees and berry bushes are planned; today saplings are world decoration only, orchard Fruit and Date clusters are inedible blocks, and Berries come only from the starter pack.","href":"features.html#f-planned-planting-food-plants","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Inventory screen and its tabs","text":"Press E (on foot) to open the inventory; it has Pack, Craft, Home and Journal tabs in survival, plus a Build tab in creative. Tab and Shift+Tab switch tabs. E: Open/close the inventory (on foot; not while in a vehicle or typing in a search/note field) Tab / Shift+Tab: Next / previous inventory tab (inventory open) Click a tab button: Switch to Pack, Craft, Build (creative), Home or Journal (inventory open)","href":"features.html#f-inventory-workspaces","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Carry capacity (mass-based pack)","text":"Your pack is limited by total weight, not slots: 80 kg, with no stack limit. Blocks are carried as half-metre pieces; a full 1 m block is 8 pieces and weighs what the block weighs.","href":"features.html#f-carry-capacity","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Pack tab: Items and Character","text":"The Pack tab lists everything you carry, sorted tools first, then food and drink, blocks, materials and spawn eggs. Click an item to use it: hold a block for placing, equip or stow a tool, eat a food, drink a flask, or equip the weapon for clicked ammunition. Left click an item: Use / select / equip it (Pack > Items) Hover + 1-9/0: Bind the hovered item to that hotbar slot (Pack > Items) Drag an item onto a hotbar slot: Bind it to that slot (inventory open) Mouse wheel / Prev / Next: Page through carried items (Pack > Items) Click Items / Character: Switch between the item list and a rotating character portrait (Pack tab)","href":"features.html#f-pack-items-list","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Item tooltips and 3D previews","text":"Hovering an item shows its name, quantity, category and click hints; the side panel shows a rotating 3D model with a description, where it comes from, what uses it, and its weight per piece.","href":"features.html#f-item-info-previews","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Ten-slot hotbar","text":"The hotbar has ten slots that you bind to item kinds; they start empty. Bind by hovering an item and pressing a number key, or by dragging; select slots with 1-9 and 0, the mouse wheel, or [ and ]. 1-9, 0: Select hotbar slot 1-10 (on foot, inventory closed) Mouse wheel / [ ]: Cycle hotbar slots (on foot, inventory closed) Hover item + 1-9/0: Bind that item to the slot (inventory open (Pack list or creative catalog)) Drag item onto slot: Bind it; drag slot onto slot swaps; drag a slot off the bar clears it (inventory open) Click an empty slot: Select it (inventory open)","href":"features.html#f-hotbar-quick-slots","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Equipping tools (Tab cycling)","text":"On foot with the inventory closed, Tab cycles through the tools and weapons you carry and back to empty hands; Shift+Tab goes backwards. Crafting a tool equips it automatically, and clicking a tool in the Pack equips or stows it. Tab / Shift+Tab: Equip next / previous carried tool, then free hands (on foot, inventory closed)","href":"features.html#f-equip-tools-tab","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Creative catalog (Build tab)","text":"In creative, the Build tab has a searchable browser of every item: click to take one, Shift-click to fill your remaining capacity, right-click to remove one carried, Shift-right-click to remove all, middle-click to star. Click item: Take one (blocks are selected, tools equipped) (creative Build tab) Shift+click item: Take as many as fit in the pack (creative Build tab) Right click / Shift+right click item: Remove one / all carried units (creative Build tab) Middle click item or Star button: Star / unstar (creative Build tab) Click search box, type; Enter: Filter by name; Enter finishes typing; right-click the box clears it (creative Build tab) Page Up / Page Down, Prev/Next, wheel over the browser: Change catalog page (creative Build tab) Hover + 1-9/0 or drag to slot: Bind the item to a hotbar slot (creative Build tab)","href":"features.html#f-creative-catalog","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Craft tab (crafting panel)","text":"Open the inventory and choose Craft. Recipes are grouped into Timber, Fire & food, Materials and Tools & arms; the panel shows each ingredient as carried / required and crafts anywhere, with no workbench, fire or furnace. Up / Down arrows, mouse wheel: Select recipe (Craft tab) Left / Right arrows or click a section: Change section (Craft tab) Enter or C, or click Craft: Craft once (Craft tab) Shift+Enter, Shift+C or Shift+click Craft: Craft repeatedly until materials or space run out (max 256 batches) (Craft tab)","href":"features.html#f-crafting-panel","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Quick craft (X)","text":"With the inventory closed, X crafts the first recipe you can currently afford, in recipe-list order, not the recipe you selected. In creative it only shows a message. X: Craft the first affordable recipe once (survival, on foot, inventory closed)","href":"features.html#f-quick-craft-x","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Complete recipe list","text":"All 37 recipes in this version. Counts are half-metre pieces (8 pieces = one full 1 m block); all are crafted from the Craft tab with no station, and creative crafting is free.","href":"features.html#f-crafting-recipes","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Interchangeable ingredients","text":"Some ingredients accept a group of materials you can mix: boards of any species, any of 26 natural stones, any hardwood log or deadwood, and plant fiber or wool. Every other ingredient must be that exact material.","href":"features.html#f-ingredient-groups","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Charcoal, cooking and glass (no furnace)","text":"Fire work is instant crafting, not a placed furnace or campfire: burn 4 boards into 2 Charcoal, then use Charcoal to cook meat, fire Pale sand into Glass, blow Glass into flasks, and fire clay, limestone and lacquer.","href":"features.html#f-fire-and-glass-chain","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Lumen-crystal recipes in survival","text":"Red lanterns, the Alder rifle, rifle rounds, TNT and dynamite all need Lumen crystal, which does not generate naturally, so survival players cannot gather enough to craft them.","href":"features.html#f-lumen-crystal-supply","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Mining and collecting blocks","text":"Hold left click on a block within 6 m. In survival a progress bar fills, then the whole 1 m block (up to 8 pieces) goes straight into your pack; hold Shift to take a single half-metre piece. Nothing drops on the ground: if the pieces do not fit, the block stays put. Hold left click: Mine the targeted block (survival) / remove it instantly (creative) (on foot, aiming within 6 m) Shift + hold left click: Mine a single half-metre piece (detail brush) (on foot)","href":"features.html#f-mining-harvesting","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Mining speed and tool effectiveness","text":"Any block can be mined by hand; a tool only speeds up its own material class. Pickaxe x4 on rock, crystal, glass, ice and masonry; hatchet x3.6 on logs, boards, leaves and other wooden blocks; shovel x3.4 on loose ground.","href":"features.html#f-mining-speed-tools","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Felling trees","text":"Cutting a log on a natural tree (or a cactus) brings down that piece and everything growing from it. In survival the chop takes the combined time of all removed wood and leaves, and all of it goes into your pack.","href":"features.html#f-tree-felling","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Stone tools (single tier, no durability)","text":"There is one tool tier: Stone sword, Stone pickaxe, Stone hatchet and Stone shovel. Tools never wear out or break.","href":"features.html#f-stone-tools","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Where crafting materials come from","text":"Natural stone and wood are everywhere; Pale sand and River clay line shores, Reeds grow in wetlands, Wild flax (for fiber) grows in meadows of new worlds, and feathers, wool and meat come from animals.","href":"features.html#f-material-sources","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Block types and material families","text":"There are 173 block types (including 6 kinds of water), all in the creative catalog; 19 of them can be crafted. Metals and gems are limited to decorative stone blocks: Iron ore chunks, Bog iron, Red ironstone, Amethyst and Lumen crystal.","href":"features.html#f-block-materials-catalogue","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Furnace, smelting, metal tools and durability","text":"A placeable furnace/campfire with cooking time, smelting, iron (and copper/bronze) tools and tool durability are planned; today cooking and glass are instant recipes and only stone tools exist.","href":"features.html#f-planned-furnace-metals","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Items on the ground, dropping and pickup feed","text":"Visible dropped items, deliberate item dropping and a corner '+3 Wood' pickup feed are planned; today mined blocks and animal drops go straight into the pack and only a status message reports them.","href":"features.html#f-planned-ground-drops","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Capacity 1000 and compact inventory grid","text":"A capacity of 1000 (labelled Capacity, not kg) and a compact icon-grid inventory with hover tooltips are planned; today the pack is 80 kg shown as boxed rows with names.","href":"features.html#f-planned-inventory-overhaul","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Doors, storage chests and furniture","text":"Doors, placeable storage chests, closets, chairs, beds, sofas, stools and extendable tables are requested but not in this version; the single Home crate is the only storage.","href":"features.html#f-planned-doors-chests-furniture","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Target outline and placement ghost","text":"Aim at a block up to 6 m away: an orange outline marks the 1 m cell a left click removes, and a see-through copy of your held block with a teal outline shows where a right click places it. A red outline alone means the block cannot go there; hold Shift and both shrink to a single half-metre voxel. Hold Shift: Outline and ghost switch to one half-metre voxel (on foot, flying or swimming) F1: Clean view hides the outline, ghost and HUD (while playing)","href":"features.html#f-block-targeting-feedback","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Placing blocks","text":"Select a block and right click to place a full 1 m cube (eight half-metre pieces) in the cell beside the face you aim at. Creative placement is free; in survival each whole block spends 8 carried pieces of that material. Right click: Place the held block at the ghost (one block per click) (block selected, no tool equipped)","href":"features.html#f-whole-block-placement","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Half-metre detail building (hold Shift)","text":"Hold Shift while placing or mining to work on one half-metre voxel, an eighth of a block, instead of a whole 1 m cube. Use it for half steps, thin walls, trim and carving; in survival each detail voxel costs or yields exactly one piece. Shift + Right click: Place a single half-metre voxel (block selected) Shift + Left click: Carve a single half-metre voxel (any)","href":"features.html#f-half-metre-detail-building","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Pick a block (middle click)","text":"In creative, middle click picks the block you aim at: if it is already in a quick slot that slot is selected, otherwise it is held without changing your slots. Middle click: Pick the targeted block (creative)","href":"features.html#f-middle-click-pick","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Building in and under water","text":"Your aim passes through water to the first solid block, so you can target the lake or sea bed and build up from it. Water, like air and plants, counts as free space and is replaced by placed blocks; you cannot click the water surface itself.","href":"features.html#f-building-in-water","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"What cannot be placed or edited","text":"Only blocks can be placed; tools, food and other items cannot. Nothing is placed inside your body, into occupied cells, in survival without enough pieces, or while a tool, bow, rifle or throwable is in hand or you are in a vehicle; the Home crate is protected and edits stop at the world's save limit.","href":"features.html#f-placement-restrictions","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Creative brushes (B)","text":"In creative, B cycles nine brushes: Block, Line, Wall, Floor, Replace, Copy, Bench, Railing and Roof. Every brush except Block builds with the block in your hand, shows the result as a ghost first, and changes at most 512 half-metre pieces (64 whole blocks) per action. B: Next brush; clears any corner selection and copied selection (creative, not holding a bow, rifle or throwable) Tool: \u003cbrush> button: Same as B (E > Build)","href":"features.html#f-creative-brush-cycle","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Line, Wall and Floor brushes","text":"Right click once to set the first corner at the ghost, aim at the far corner (the ghost previews the whole shape) and right click again to build. Line follows the longest direction, Wall stands upright along the longer horizontal direction, and Floor stays level at the first corner's height. Right click: First click sets the corner; second click builds (creative, Line/Wall/Floor) Hold Shift on the first click: Build in half-metre steps instead of whole blocks (creative) B: Cancel the selection (moves to the next brush) (creative)","href":"features.html#f-brush-line-wall-floor","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Replace brush","text":"Aim at a block of the material you want to swap and right click, then aim at the opposite corner block and right click again. Every block of that first material inside the box becomes the block in your hand; everything else is left alone. Right click on a block: Set the first corner and the material to replace (creative, Replace) Right click on the opposite corner block: Replace matching blocks in the box (creative, Replace)","href":"features.html#f-brush-replace","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Copy brush (copy and paste)","text":"Aim at one corner block and right click, then aim at the opposite corner block and right click to copy everything solid in that box. Each further right click pastes a copy with its lowest corner at the ghost; R turns it 90 degrees and B discards the copy. Right click, then right click: Copy the box between two aimed blocks (creative, Copy) Right click: Paste at the ghost (repeatable) (after copying) R: Rotate the paste 90 degrees (creative, Copy) Hold Shift: Position the paste on the half-metre grid instead of whole-block positions (creative, Copy)","href":"features.html#f-brush-copy-paste","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Bench, Railing and Roof patterns","text":"These brushes stamp a small ready-made shape from the block in your hand at the ghost with a single right click; press R first to turn it 90 degrees. Right click: Stamp the pattern at the ghost (creative, Bench/Railing/Roof) R: Rotate the pattern 90 degrees (creative)","href":"features.html#f-brush-bench-railing-roof","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Undo and redo building","text":"In creative, Ctrl+Z undoes and Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes your building in this session: brush actions, single blocks placed, and blocks or trees mined. Undo changes nothing if those cells were altered since, if it would put a block inside you, or at the save edit limit. Ctrl+Z: Undo the last building action (creative) Ctrl+Shift+Z: Redo (creative) Undo / Redo buttons: Same as the keys (E > Build)","href":"features.html#f-build-undo-redo","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Build tab and material palettes","text":"In creative, E > Build holds the searchable item catalog plus buttons: Tool (next brush), Undo, Redo, four preset palettes and Save palette. A preset replaces all ten quick slots with a themed set of blocks; Save palette stores your current ten slots and Shift-clicking it loads them back. Click Timber / Masonry / Coastal / Garden: Fill the ten quick slots with that preset and select slot 1 (E > Build, creative) Click Save palette: Store the current ten quick-slot bindings (E > Build) Shift + click Save palette: Load the stored palette into the quick slots (E > Build) Click Star / Unstar: Add or remove the inspected item from the STARRED list (E > Build)","href":"features.html#f-build-workspace-palettes","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"TNT: place and ignite (G)","text":"Place TNT like any block; a whole block holds 8 charges and a Shift-placed half-metre piece holds 1. Aim at it and press G: it and every TNT piece touching it (even diagonally) light together on a 2.6-second fuse, pop loose, and can fall, roll or float before exploding. G: Ignite the aimed TNT and all TNT touching it (aiming at TNT within 6 m, single-player) Right click: Place TNT (Shift: one half-metre charge) (TNT selected) Left click: Mine TNT back without setting it off (any)","href":"features.html#f-tnt-place-and-ignite","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Explosions: reach, damage and terrain","text":"One whole TNT block blasts about 4.25 m; touching groups explode as one bigger blast (up to 10.5 m), while a single half-metre charge or dynamite stick reaches only about 1.5 m. Survival players near a blast lose health and are knocked back (creative players are unharmed), creatures are hurt in both modes, and blasted blocks are destroyed, not collected.","href":"features.html#f-explosion-effects","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Throwing dynamite","text":"Select dynamite, hold right click to wind up and release to throw; a longer wind-up throws farther. The fuse starts on release and the stick goes off 2.3 s later where it stops, sticking to the first surface it hits (walls and ceilings included), as a small fixed blast of about 1.5 m. Right click (hold, then release): Wind up and throw a dynamite stick (dynamite selected)","href":"features.html#f-dynamite-throwing","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Mechanical building (switches, doors, machines)","text":"There are no doors, switches, levers, pistons or item-moving machines to build.","href":"features.html#f-mechanical-building","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"World generations (terrain rule versions)","text":"Each world keeps the terrain rules it was created with. Worlds made with Create world use generation 10; older saves keep their older generation, so game updates never reshape existing builds. To see newer terrain, create a new world.","href":"features.html#f-world-generation-version","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"World seeds (players cannot choose one)","text":"Create world asks only for a name and picks a random seed from the clock. You cannot enter or view a seed. The default first world always uses the same fixed meadow-island seed, so every player's default world has the same terrain. Only the developer debug session can set a seed.","href":"features.html#f-world-seed-selection","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"92 biomes","text":"Worlds contain 92 named biomes, from meadows, orchards and bamboo thickets to salt flats, kelp forests, meteor fields and surface lava tubes. All 92 can generate naturally, and the map locator can find any of them.","href":"features.html#f-biomes-92","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Endless archipelago and the starting island","text":"The world is an endless sea of island groups. You start at X 0, Z 0 on a large island several kilometres across, in a protected, gentle meadow clearing. Other islands range from small islets and palm-ringed atolls to large islands and occasional continent-sized landmasses. Open ocean separates island groups, and satellite islets sit at least 240 m offshore.","href":"features.html#f-archipelago-and-starting-island","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Hillside caves","text":"Some hills have cave entrances that lead into one of three layouts: branching passages around a chamber, a looping gallery around a central rock mass, or a broad vaulted hall held up by four rock pillars. Floors climb in walkable half-metre steps. Caves are single, bounded sites rather than deep cave networks.","href":"features.html#f-caves","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Landmarks: meteor craters, amethyst gorges, lava tubes and other geological…","text":"Rare large sites appear across the world. Impact fields have a main crater bowl with a raised ejecta rim plus two smaller craters. Amethyst gorges are winding canyons through crystal-studded rock. On volcanic islands, cooled lava tubes are walkable roofed passages with two open skylights. Other sites include hot-spring terraces, geyser basins, oases, tepui mesas, ice spires, granite domes, karst towers, moraines and limestone pavements.","href":"features.html#f-geological-landmarks","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Volcanoes and volcanic terrain","text":"Some islands carry shield volcanoes, steep stratovolcanoes or breached calderas. Older ones are eroded and overgrown. Volcanic ground has basalt, obsidian, ash, lava rock, pahoehoe and glowing magma stone, and nearby geothermal areas have warm and boiling pools and steam vents. Everything is solid and cooled: there is no molten lava, and nothing burns.","href":"features.html#f-volcanoes","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Rivers, lakes and wetlands","text":"Islands have river channels, inland lakes up to 62 m above sea level, marshes, bogs, mangroves, bayous and frozen lakes. All natural water is still: rivers do not flow or push you. The kind of water depends on the place: ocean water, fresh water in lakes and rivers, black water in warm wetlands, warm or boiling mineral water at hot springs, dark fjord water, and ice on frozen lakes.","href":"features.html#f-rivers-lakes-wetlands","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Natural waterfalls and dripstone caves","text":"There are no natural waterfalls, stalactites, stalagmites or dripstone. Water you place in creative can pour over an edge, but there are no cascade visuals or spray, and the waterfall sound loop is unused.","href":"features.html#f-waterfalls-dripstone","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Expedition sites and one-time supply finds","text":"Generation-10 worlds have three small landmarks within about 300 m of spawn: the Survey workshop to the east, the Reed refuge to the south-west and the Stone overlook to the north-east. Click one in the Journal to make it your destination. Walk to within 12 m of it on foot to discover it, then press Find in the Journal while still nearby to collect its one-time supplies. E, then Tab to Journal (or the Journal button on the…: Open the Journal with the three expedition sites listed ('?' undiscovered, '+' discovered) (in the world) Click a site name: Make it your destination (map marker, light beacon and on-screen marker) (Journal) Find button: Collect the site's supplies; it shows 'Taken' afterwards (Journal, within 12 m of a discovered site, on foot)","href":"features.html#f-expedition-sites","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Day and night","text":"A full day and night lasts 20 real minutes: the sun is up for about 10 minutes and down for about 10. New worlds start just after sunrise. Night is darker with cool lighting and a moon, but nothing dangerous happens at night, and there is no way to sleep or skip it. Time of day is saved with the world.","href":"features.html#f-day-night-cycle","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Automatic weather","text":"Weather moves through a fixed 30-minute pattern: clear, cloudy, rain, cloudy, partly cloudy, foggy, cloudy, rainstorm, thunderstorm, cloudy, about 3 minutes each, blending smoothly. Each world starts at a different point in the pattern. Weather is the same everywhere in a world, so rain also falls over deserts and there is no snowfall. Rain stops at roofs, thunderstorms flash with lightning and delayed thunder, and lightning causes no damage or fires.","href":"features.html#f-automatic-weather","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Time, weather and season controls (creative)","text":"In creative, press Esc and open Settings > World to set Time of day (Cycle, Dawn, Day, Dusk, Night), Weather (Auto, Clear, Partly, Cloudy, Rain, Storm, Thunder, Foggy) and Season (Auto, Spring, Summer, Autumn, Winter). In survival these rows only display the current state. Esc, then the World tab: Open the world controls (in the world) Click a chip or use the arrows: Hold a time, weather or season, or return to automatic (creative)","href":"features.html#f-creative-world-controls","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Seasons","text":"A year is 80 real minutes, about 20 minutes per season, and new worlds start in summer. Seasons tint grass, deciduous leaves, needles, blossoms, water, sky and sunlight, for example russet autumn foliage and a cooler winter light. Leaves never thin out and nothing turns snowy. In survival, seasons also shift the air temperature, from +7 degrees in summer to -7 in winter, so they are not purely visual yet.","href":"features.html#f-seasons","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Fire","text":"There is no fire you can light, spread or put out. The crafting 'FIRE & FOOD' tab only covers recipes such as charcoal, cooking and kilns. Explosions show a brief flash and burst but leave nothing burning.","href":"features.html#f-fire","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Lava","text":"There is no lava liquid. Volcanoes and lava tubes are made of cooled, solid rock: basalt, obsidian, lava rock, pahoehoe and glowing but harmless magma stone. Nothing flows, burns or cools into stone.","href":"features.html#f-flowing-lava","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Compact map","text":"A small north-up terrain map sits in the top-right corner and is on by default. It shows 128 m around you with your heading arrow, saved places, your destination and any locator highlight. Press M to hide or show it. M: Show or hide the compact map (in the world)","href":"features.html#f-compact-map","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Fullscreen world map","text":"Shift+M opens a north-up world map, 512 m across by default. The mouse wheel zooms from 128 m to about 131 km across, and dragging looks around without moving you. A readout under the map gives the cursor's X/Z and its distance from you. The view returns to you each time the map opens, and the world pauses while the map is open. Shift+M: Open or close the fullscreen map (in the world) Esc: Close the fullscreen map M: Close the fullscreen map and hide the compact map (fullscreen map, when no text field is focused) Mouse wheel over the map: Zoom in or out one step (fullscreen map) Left-drag: Pan the view (a drag starts after 6 pixels) (fullscreen map) BACK TO PLAYER button: Recentre the view on you (fullscreen map side panel) Journal button: Close the map and open the Journal (fullscreen map)","href":"features.html#f-fullscreen-map","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Map locator: find biomes and landmarks","text":"The panel left of the fullscreen map lists Volcano, Cliff island and Mountain range, then all 92 biomes alphabetically. Type to filter the list, then click a result or press Enter. The search spirals outward from the map centre, recentres and zooms the map on the first match, highlights matching terrain in cyan and drops a pin with distance and coordinates. It never moves you. Click the search field and type: Filter places by name or alias (e.g. crystal, meteorite, caldera, lava tunnel) (fullscreen map) Enter: Search for an exact name match, otherwise the first entry on the current page (search field focused) Click a list entry: Start searching for that place (fullscreen map) Mouse wheel over the list, or PREV/NEXT: Change list page (fullscreen map) FIND NEXT: Continue outward to a match more than about 1 km from the last one (after a match) CLEAR: Remove the search, pin and cyan highlight (fullscreen map)","href":"features.html#f-map-locator","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Teleport by coordinates (creative)","text":"In creative, type X Z in the fullscreen map's COORDINATES (METRES) field and press Enter or TELEPORT to land on a safe surface spot. Type X Y Z for an exact position. In survival the button reads CREATIVE MODE ONLY. Click COORDINATES field, type X Z or X Y Z: Enter a destination in metres (spaces or commas; negatives and decimals allowed) (fullscreen map, creative) Enter or TELEPORT: Travel there (fullscreen map, creative, not in a vehicle) Ctrl+Backspace: Clear the coordinates field (coordinates field focused)","href":"features.html#f-map-coordinate-teleport","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Click the map to travel (creative)","text":"In creative, click (without dragging) anywhere on the fullscreen map to travel to a clear landing spot there. In survival a click only shows a reminder, because the map is a chart only. You must leave any vehicle or boat helm first. Left-click on the map (no drag): Travel to that column (fullscreen map, creative, not controlling a vehicle)","href":"features.html#f-map-click-travel","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Right-click map markers","text":"Right-click a spot on the fullscreen map to save a numbered 'Marker N' place there and make it your destination. Right-click on an existing pin to delete it. Markers share the Journal's 16-place limit and can be renamed in the Journal. Right-click on the map: Add 'Marker N' at that spot and set it as the destination, or remove the pin under the cursor (fullscreen map)","href":"features.html#f-map-right-click-markers","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Journal places and notes","text":"The Journal stores up to 16 named places, each with a short note, and lists them with their distance from you. 'Pin this location' saves where you stand, using the Name and Note fields. Select a place to edit it or make it your destination. Places appear as small amber squares on both maps, with names on the fullscreen map. E, then Tab to Journal, or the map's Journal button: Open the Journal (in the world) Click Name / Note fields and type; Enter: Edit the text; Enter ends typing (Journal) Pin this location: Save your current position (blank name becomes 'Place N') and make it the destination (Journal) Click a place: Select it, load its name/note and make it the destination (Journal) Save selected note: Update the selected place's name and note (Journal) Remove selected pin: Delete the selected place and clear the destination (Journal) Prev / Next: Page through places (Journal)","href":"features.html#f-journal-places","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Destinations: map marker, light beacon and on-screen marker","text":"Your chosen destination (an expedition site, a Journal place or a map marker) shows as an amber '+' on both maps, an amber light column 320 m tall in the world, and a gold diamond on screen with its name and distance ('HERE' within 3 m). The diamond sticks to the screen edge when the destination is out of view. With no destination chosen, the maps show your home crate instead (map '+' only, no beacon).","href":"features.html#f-destination-beacon","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Player arrow on the map","text":"On both maps you are shown as a gold arrowhead with a dark outline, pointing the way you face. It is hidden when you are outside the visible area, for example after panning away. N, S, E and W labels mark the edges, and the fullscreen map has a scale bar.","href":"features.html#f-map-player-marker","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"The map in survival (chart only, no fog of war)","text":"In survival the maps show the whole generated world, including places you have never visited. You can zoom, drag, search with the locator and set markers, but clicking does not travel and coordinate teleport is creative-only.","href":"features.html#f-survival-map-chart","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Coordinates and biome readout","text":"A small top-right readout shows FPS, your X, Y and Z in metres and the biome you are in. It is on by default each session and can be turned off with Settings > Debug > FPS and coordinates. It is hidden while panels are open.","href":"features.html#f-position-readout","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Launching and packing up a sailing boat","text":"Select the Small sailing boat and right-click open water within 6 m to launch it. It floats on any water: sea, lakes and all six water types, at their real surface height. To carry it again, look at an unoccupied boat within 6 m and Shift-right-click, with 18 kg of free carrying capacity. Survival uses up and returns the boat item; creative launches from the catalog without using one. Right-click open water: Launch the selected boat (Small sailing boat selected, water within 6 m) Shift + right-click: Pack up the boat you are looking at (unoccupied boat within 6 m, any item held)","href":"features.html#f-sailing-launch-and-pack","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Sailing: deck, helm, sails, steering and camera","text":"Walk or jump onto the boat's wooden deck like normal ground. Press V within 9 m to take the helm, and press V again to step back onto the cockpit. At the helm, hold W to raise the sails and S to reef them, press Space to lower them fully, and steer with A/D. Press C or F5 to switch between first-person and chase view, and hold Alt for free look. V: Take or leave the helm (within 9 m of the boat or on its deck) W (hold): Raise the sail setting; the sails follow over about 2 s (at the helm) S (hold): Reef (lower the sail setting) (at the helm) Space: Lower the sails completely (at the helm) A / D: Steer left / right (rudder eases in and recentres) (at the helm) C or F5: Switch first-person / chase camera (at the helm) Hold Alt: Free look (at the helm)","href":"features.html#f-sailing-helm-controls","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Sailing: wind, speed and tacking","text":"Each world has a fixed prevailing wind direction, and its strength changes with the weather: about 2.8 m/s in clear weather up to 8.5 m/s in thunderstorms. Running with the wind is fastest, and across the wind gives about two-thirds of the wind speed. Within 45 degrees of sailing straight into the wind the sails give no push, so zig-zag (tack) to go upwind. The red masthead pennant and the HUD flag (bow at the top) point downwind.","href":"features.html#f-sailing-wind-and-tacking","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Boats and saving","text":"Up to 8 launched boats are saved with their position, heading and health. If you save while at the helm, you reload standing in the stopped boat's cockpit rather than at the helm.","href":"features.html#f-sailing-saving","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Flowing water (placing and blocking water)","text":"In creative, the catalog's Water block is a flowing source: it spreads up to 7 half-metre cells sideways and pours down over edges. Any block placed into water displaces it, and digging next to the sea or a lake lets water flow into the gap. Flowing water never recedes. There are no buckets, survival players cannot pick up or place water, and the other five water types are still blocks that do not flow. Right-click with Water selected: Place a flowing water source (creative) Right-click with any block aimed into water: Replace that water cell with the block (building)","href":"features.html#f-flowing-water","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Water entry splashes and bubbles","text":"Stepping or falling into water plays a splash sound and throws droplets from the surface. Moving through water releases bubbles and a swim sound about every 1.4 m. Rain makes small ripple splashes on surfaces. Boats leave no bow spray or wake.","href":"features.html#f-water-splashes","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Animal drops","text":"In survival, an animal you kill puts its drops straight into your pack. Meat and venison are food, feathers make arrows, and wool can replace plant fiber; hide currently has no use.","href":"features.html#f-animal-drops","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Peaceful world: no hostile mobs","text":"All animals are passive: they never attack, chase or damage you.","href":"features.html#f-no-hostile-mobs","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Hitting animals (melee)","text":"Left-click an animal within reach to hit it. In survival a bare hand or ordinary tool deals 12 damage and the Stone sword 28; in creative one hit removes the animal (without drops). A hurt animal runs away for a few seconds. Left click: Hit the aimed animal (on foot)","href":"features.html#f-melee-attacks","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Alder bow and arrows","text":"Equip the bow, hold right click to draw (full draw takes 0.85 s) and release to shoot; letting go before about a fifth of a draw cancels the shot. Longer draws fly faster and hit harder. In survival each shot uses one arrow and arrows are not recovered; creative arrows are unlimited. Hold right click, release: Draw and shoot (bow equipped) Hold Shift: Sneak to steady aim (bow equipped) Tab / Shift+Tab: Cycle carried tools and weapons (on foot)","href":"features.html#f-alder-bow","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Alder rifle and rifle rounds","text":"Left click fires; hold right click to raise the sights for less spread and sway. After every shot the rifle reloads itself in about one second; R does not reload it (in survival R eats food instead). Survival uses one rifle round per shot; creative is unlimited. Left click: Fire (rifle equipped) Hold right click: Aim through the sights (rifle equipped, not reloading) Hold Shift: Sneak to steady aim (rifle equipped)","href":"features.html#f-alder-rifle","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Throwing rocks","text":"Equip throwing rocks, hold right click to wind up (full wind-up in 0.7 s) and release to throw; a too-short wind-up is cancelled. Harder throws fly farther and hit harder. Each throw uses one rock in survival. Hold right click, release: Wind up and throw (rocks equipped)","href":"features.html#f-throwing-rocks","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Steadier aim for ranged weapons","text":"Ranged spread depends on how you stand: sneaking (Shift) tightens it, moving or being in the air widens it, a fuller bow draw or the rifle's raised sights steady it, and each shot briefly opens the reticle.","href":"features.html#f-aim-steadiness","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"94 animal species","text":"The game has 94 animal species, from cows, sheep and rabbits to whales, giant squid, triops, bats, otters, penguins and swans. Durability ranges from 2 HP (fly, ant, butterfly, banana slug, comb jelly, triops) to 240 HP (whale). There are no camels.","href":"features.html#f-animal-species-roster","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Where and how many animals appear","text":"Animals appear around you according to the biome: each biome has its own small group of species and every one of the 94 can appear somewhere; swans turn up in wetlands and deltas, fish only in deep enough water. Up to 36 wild animals are around you at once, with per-species limits; animals more than 180 m away are removed and new ones appear as you explore. Animals are not saved.","href":"features.html#f-animal-natural-spawning","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Animal spawn eggs (creative)","text":"The creative catalog's MOBS tab has a spawn egg for each of the 94 species. Taking one equips it; hold right click to wind up and release to throw, and the animal hatches where the egg bursts. Eggs cannot be crafted. Hold right click, release: Throw the egg; the animal hatches on impact (egg equipped)","href":"features.html#f-animal-spawn-eggs","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Animal behaviour","text":"Farm animals stay calm near you; shy wild species (rabbits, foxes, frogs, many birds, horses, zebras, giraffes, big cats, wolves, crocodiles and others) keep about 5 m away (frogs 3 m) and run if you come closer. Any hurt animal bolts for a few seconds. Many animals pause to graze and idle animals turn their heads to watch you. Deer, rabbits, frogs, kangaroos and jerboas bound while fleeing; goats, deer and gazelles jump ledges up to 1 m; otters, penguins and swans swim and fish stay in water.","href":"features.html#f-animal-behaviour","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Animal calls","text":"25 species, including cows, pigs, sheep, goats, geese, swans, wolves, bears, lions, tigers and crows, make calls you can hear within about 24 m; blue jays and crows call when woodland birds are active.","href":"features.html#f-animal-calls","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Taming, breeding and riding animals","text":"Animals cannot be tamed, bred, ridden, fed, milked or sheared yet. Breeding, taming and in-world drops are listed for a later milestone.","href":"features.html#f-animal-taming-breeding","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Held blocks and hands (first person)","text":"A selected block is shown as a small textured cube gripped in your right hand; tools are held on the right and the bow left of centre with a separate string hand. Empty hands show nothing, and in survival a block you no longer carry disappears from your hand.","href":"features.html#f-held-items-first-person","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Character tab (portrait only)","text":"Press E, stay on Pack and click Character to see a 3D portrait of your explorer; move the pointer across it to turn the model. It shows Creative or your health and what you are holding; there are no appearance, outfit or skin options. Click Items / Character: Switch the Pack view between your items and the portrait (E > Pack) Move the pointer over the portrait: Turn the explorer (Character subtab)","href":"features.html#f-character-tab-portrait","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Developer inspection and gallery tools","text":"Developers can browse, place and capture models and textures in a disposable headless session and export creature, gear and character galleries. These are review tools: they edit nothing, write no saves and are absent from normal builds.","href":"features.html#f-developer-asset-inspection","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Player skins and skin editor","text":"Changing how your explorer looks (skins, import/export or painting) is requested but not in the game. Everyone uses the same built-in outfit.","href":"features.html#f-player-skins","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Animal Workshop, painter and animation preview","text":"There is no in-game animal editor, animal painter, pixel-density control or live pose preview. Animals are built into the game's code, and only developers can export them for review.","href":"features.html#f-animal-workshop-painter","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Structure Workshop and player blueprints","text":"You cannot save your own builds as blueprints, import or export them, or place one with a movable ghost and a materials list in survival. The nearest tools today are the creative Copy brush (512 half-metre cells, this session only).","href":"features.html#f-structure-workshop-blueprints","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Shader quality: None, Fast, Cinematic","text":"Settings > General > Shader picks the lighting tier. None is plain voxel lighting for weak hardware; Fast (default) adds sun shadows, sun rays and haze; Cinematic refines shadows and rays and adds reflective water at extra GPU cost.","href":"features.html#f-shader-tiers","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Horizon distance","text":"Settings > General > Horizon distance sets how far low-detail distant terrain reaches: 8, 16, 32, 48, 64 (default), 96, 128, 192, 256 or 512 chunks. A chunk is 16 m, so 512 reaches about 8 km. The setting's tooltip notes that nearby full-detail blocks stay at nine chunks.","href":"features.html#f-horizon-distance","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Frame limit","text":"Settings > General > Frame limit caps the frame rate at 60 (default), 120, 240 or Unlimited. The game does not use vsync, so this setting is what paces frames; Study Grove always runs at about 30 FPS.","href":"features.html#f-frame-limit","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Atmospheric fog (haze) presets","text":"Settings > General > Atmospheric fog chooses Light, Balanced (default) or Dense distance haze; Dense hides far terrain. Haze strength scales with your horizon distance.","href":"features.html#f-atmospheric-fog","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Field of view","text":"Settings > General > Field of view ranges from 60 to 96 degrees in 2-degree steps (default 72). Wider values show more without changing movement speed.","href":"features.html#f-field-of-view","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"GUI size","text":"Settings > General > GUI size scales HUD text and menus: Auto (default, adapts from 800x450 up to 4K), Small, Normal or Large.","href":"features.html#f-gui-size","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Voxel view: block edges and X-ray outlines","text":"Settings > Debug > Voxel view switches between Off (default), Edges, which tints every block border, and X-ray, which draws only the outlines.","href":"features.html#f-voxel-view","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Distant terrain on/off","text":"Settings > Debug > Distant terrain (on by default) draws the low-detail horizon beyond nearby chunks. Turning it off saves work on slow machines; the view then ends at the loaded terrain and new chunks can appear at that edge during fast travel.","href":"features.html#f-distant-terrain-toggle","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Chunk fade-in","text":"Settings > General > Chunk fade-in (on by default) makes newly loaded terrain fade in over 0.6 seconds; turn it off to make terrain appear at once. Terrain prepared on the loading screen is already fully visible.","href":"features.html#f-chunk-fade-in","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Performance overlay (F3)","text":"Press F3 (or use Settings > Debug > Performance) to show a CPU-effort pie for the last half second, frame time against your frame budget, the worst recent frame with a spike count, and the lag-spike log status. Red marks work using more than one CPU core or over half the frame budget. F3: Show/hide the performance overlay (in a world)","href":"features.html#f-performance-overlay","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Chunk borders (F7), LOD view (F8) and all diagnostics (F9)","text":"F7 draws 16 m chunk borders with the current chunk in gold. F8 cycles LOD view Off, Tint, Colors: cyan is detailed terrain, and green, blue, yellow, orange, purple and red mark the 0.5 to 16 m distant stages. F9 turns all three diagnostics off if any is on, otherwise all on with Tint. F7: Toggle chunk borders (in a world) F8: Cycle LOD view Off / Tint / Colors (in a world) F9: All diagnostics off, or all on (Tint) (in a world)","href":"features.html#f-diagnostic-overlays","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Live GPU timing in the performance overlay","text":"Planned: real GPU pass timings in the F3 overlay during normal play. Today the overlay's GPU box says 'GPU timing unavailable in this view'.","href":"features.html#f-gpu-timing-live","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"1024-chunk horizon (about 16 km)","text":"Planned: a selectable 1024-chunk horizon. The largest option today is 512 chunks.","href":"features.html#f-horizon-1024","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Keep the display awake while playing","text":"Not yet: the game does not stop your screen from dimming or locking, so your system's power settings apply, including during long Study Grove sessions.","href":"features.html#f-display-awake","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Master volume","text":"Settings > Audio > Master volume sets music, sound effects and nature sounds together from 0% (mute) to 100% (default) and applies immediately.","href":"features.html#f-master-volume","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Music: on/off, speed and song choice","text":"Settings > Audio lets you switch Music on or off (sound effects keep playing), set Music speed from 0.5x to 2.0x in 0.1x steps (tempo and pitch change together) and pick the Previous or Next song. The playlist then advances by itself and loops.","href":"features.html#f-music-player","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Nature ambience","text":"Wind, rain (including rain drumming on a roof above you), storms, forest, caves, birds, night wildlife, ponds, shorelines and streams fade in and out around you automatically. Ambience goes quiet in menus and underwater and follows the master volume.","href":"features.html#f-nature-ambience","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Dedicated credits music","text":"Planned: an original piece that plays only on the Credits screen. Today the normal playlist keeps playing during the credits.","href":"features.html#f-credits-music","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Now playing display with album art","text":"Planned: a compact album-art thumbnail beside the current song title. Today the song title appears only on the Settings Audio tab and in Study Grove.","href":"features.html#f-now-playing-album-art","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Study Grove focus timer","text":"Press P in a world, or choose Study timer on the main menu, to open Study Grove: a large countdown over an animated voxel landscape. It starts with 25 minutes of focus and 5 minutes of break, chimes between phases and gives a reminder 30 seconds before a break ends. The game is paused while it is open. P: Open Study Grove (pauses gameplay) (in a world or on the main menu) Space: Start / pause the timer (Study Grove) Esc or Back: Leave Study Grove and pause the timer Tab or Arrow keys, then Enter: Select and press a button (Study Grove)","href":"features.html#f-study-grove","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Study over your own world (Real terrain backdrop)","text":"In Study Grove choose Backdrop: Real terrain to study over your actual world and builds. Camera: Your view keeps your current viewpoint; Slow orbit circles gently about 12 m around it above the treetops. Weather previews World, Clear, Rain, Mist or Sunset without changing the world's saved weather, and Options shows all timer controls over the compact timer.","href":"features.html#f-study-grove-real-terrain","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"System requirements and supported platforms","text":"Linux with a Vulkan-capable integrated or discrete GPU and an X11 or XWayland session is the main, tested platform. Installers for Windows 10/11 (64-bit Intel/AMD) and macOS (Apple Silicon or Intel with a Metal GPU) are included but have not yet been checked with real graphics and sound on those systems.","href":"features.html#f-system-requirements","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Install on Linux (install.sh)","text":"From the extracted source folder run `bash install.sh` as your normal user. It installs compiler and audio tools (apt-get, dnf or pacman, using sudo only for system packages), Rust stable, builds the game and adds Voxelgame to your application menu. Setup never starts the game or touches existing worlds. bash install.sh [--yes] [--prefix PATH]…: Run setup; --yes accepts the plan, --prefix changes the app folder, --skip-prerequisites uses tools you installed yourself, --help changes nothing (terminal in the extracted source folder)","href":"features.html#f-install-linux","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Install on macOS (Install-macOS.command)","text":"Double-click Install-macOS.command, press Return to accept the plan, finish Apple's Command Line Tools installer if offered, and enter your Mac password if Homebrew asks. Setup installs Homebrew and FFmpeg when needed plus Rust, builds the game for your Mac, and creates Voxelgame.app in your home folder's Applications folder.","href":"features.html#f-install-macos","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Install on Windows 10/11 (install-windows.cmd)","text":"Double-click install-windows.cmd, confirm the plan and approve the Microsoft compiler installer if Windows asks. Setup downloads Microsoft C++ Build Tools, Rust and FFmpeg when missing, builds the game and adds Voxelgame to the Start menu. If the compiler setup asks for a restart, restart Windows and run the installer again. install-windows.ps1 -Yes / -Prefix PATH /…: Same options as the Linux/macOS installer (PowerShell in the extracted folder)","href":"features.html#f-install-windows","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Where the game, worlds and logs are stored","text":"Installed games keep worlds and settings in a per-user data folder that survives upgrades: Linux ~/.local/share/voxelgame/data, macOS ~/Library/Application Support/Voxelgame, Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Voxelgame\\data. Worlds are in the worlds folder inside it. A source checkout started with./run.sh keeps its worlds in the source folder.","href":"features.html#f-data-locations","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Updating an installed game","text":"There is no separate updater: close the game, extract the newer source folder and run its installer again. Worlds and settings stay in the data folder, a failed build leaves the old version working, and the launcher switches to the new version only after it is completely copied.","href":"features.html#f-update-by-reinstall","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Uninstalling","text":"There is no uninstaller. Delete the app folder and its menu entry (Linux ~/.local/share/applications/voxelgame.desktop, Windows Start menu 'Voxelgame' shortcut). Your worlds stay in the data folder until you delete it yourself.","href":"features.html#f-uninstall","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Build and run from source (cargo or ./run.sh)","text":"On a prepared Linux machine with Rust 1.75 or newer, run `cargo run --release --locked --bin voxelgame`, or `./run.sh`, which rebuilds only when sources changed and shows a small 'Preparing the game' window while it builds. Started this way, worlds live in the source folder's worlds directory.","href":"features.html#f-build-from-source","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Loading screen","text":"When the game opens, a tumbling wireframe cube settles into the Voxelgame logo above the current loading stage and an activity bar; later world loads show just the stage and bar. The terrain right around you is prepared before play starts and distant terrain keeps streaming in afterwards.","href":"features.html#f-loading-screen","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Opening the game twice","text":"Only one copy runs per user. Launching Voxelgame again brings the open window to the front instead of starting a second copy that could also write saves; the game then says 'Voxelgame was already open; click the world to recapture the mouse'.","href":"features.html#f-single-instance","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"World save files","text":"Each world is a single.vxw file inside the worlds folder. The starting world is worlds/debug/debug.vxw and is listed as DEBUG. Creating a world with a name makes worlds/\u003cname>/world.vxw plus a name.txt label; leaving the name empty makes worlds/world-0001/world.vxw, world-0002 and so on.","href":"features.html#f-world-files","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Which world Continue opens after a restart","text":"Every time the game starts, the active world is the starting world worlds/debug/debug.vxw (its name, DEBUG, is shown under the title), so Continue opens that world after a restart. To keep playing another world, open Worlds and load it.","href":"features.html#f-startup-active-world","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Saving: autosave, F6 and quitting safely","text":"The world saves when you press F6, choose Main menu or Save & quit in the Esc menu, press Ctrl+Q, close the window, choose Quit on the main menu, or load or create another world. Every 30 seconds autosave saves if the world changed. If saving fails while quitting, the game stays open so nothing is lost. F6: Save now ('World saved safely') (in a world) Ctrl+Q: Save and quit (in game) Esc > Main menu / Save & quit: Save, then return to the main menu or close (Settings panel in a world)","href":"features.html#f-saving","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"What a world save keeps","text":"A save keeps your position and view, game mode, health, hunger, thirst, stamina and temperature, inventory, quick-slot bindings and selection, equipped tool, every block you changed (with water state), time/weather/season controls, home crate and rest point, one saved build palette, journal places and expedition finds, the map destination, sailing boats and your settings. Animals, projectiles, primed TNT and Study Grove timers are not saved.","href":"features.html#f-save-contents","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Settings are stored inside each world","text":"Graphics, audio and GUI settings are saved inside each world file. A new world copies your current settings; loading another world switches to that world's own settings.","href":"features.html#f-per-world-settings","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"World size limits","text":"Each world file is capped at 8 MiB and 200,000 changed half-metre blocks. From 190,000 changes the game repeats a warning ('saving stops at 200000'), and building refuses changes beyond the budget with 'Save edit budget reached'.","href":"features.html#f-save-limits","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Older and newer saves","text":"Worlds keep the terrain generator they were created with, so old builds and untouched land stay together; new worlds use generation 10. Worlds from before the half-metre block grid are refused ('a new world is required'), and a world saved by a newer version of the game is refused by an older one.","href":"features.html#f-save-compatibility","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"When a world cannot be loaded","text":"A damaged or incompatible world file is never overwritten. If you pick it in Worlds, the menu shows 'world could not be loaded:...' and you stay where you were. If the world the game opens at startup cannot be read, the game closes and prints the reason only to a terminal.","href":"features.html#f-unreadable-save","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Backing up worlds","text":"There is no backup button yet. Each world is one folder (world.vxw plus an optional name.txt) inside the worlds folder.","href":"features.html#f-world-backups","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Choose a save file or play without saving (VOXELGAME_SAVE_PATH, --no-save)","text":"Advanced: set VOXELGAME_SAVE_PATH to start on a specific.vxw file (relative or absolute path), or start the game with --no-save to play without ever writing the world.","href":"features.html#f-save-path-override","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Installer logs","text":"Every setup run keeps a transcript, including failures: Linux ~/.local/state/voxelgame/install-\u003cdate>-\u003cid>.log, macOS ~/Library/Logs/Voxelgame, Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Voxelgame\\logs. The log path is printed at the start and end of setup.","href":"features.html#f-setup-logs","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Startup and crash error messages","text":"When the game cannot start, for example with no usable GPU, an unreadable starting world or a mistyped option, it closes and prints the reason to the terminal ('voxelgame failed:...' or 'voxelgame could not start:...'). Menu and desktop launches show no error window, so start the launcher from a terminal to see the message.","href":"features.html#f-startup-error-reporting","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Native error window","text":"Planned: a window showing the exact fatal error with details you can copy. Not in this version; errors only go to the terminal.","href":"features.html#f-native-error-window","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Lag-spike log","text":"While the Performance overlay (F3) is on, frames slower than twice your frame budget (at least 25 ms) are recorded with their slowest causes in lag-spikes.jsonl under ~/.local/state/voxelgame (or $XDG_STATE_HOME/voxelgame). Turn F3 off to stop recording.","href":"features.html#f-lag-spike-log","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Graphics driver and display problems","text":"On Linux the game needs a working Vulkan driver and an X11 or XWayland session; it has no native Wayland window. It picks your high-performance GPU and falls back automatically to a software renderer such as llvmpipe, which is fine for diagnosis but too slow for normal play.","href":"features.html#f-troubleshoot-graphics","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Sound and music requirements","text":"Sound effects play through aplay on Linux (alsa-utils) and ffplay on Windows and macOS; music also needs ffmpeg to decode the songs. The installers set these up. Without the player program the game runs silently; without ffmpeg, Music switches itself off and the status line shows 'Music unavailable:...'.","href":"features.html#f-audio-requirements","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Join a dedicated server (--connect)","text":"There is no Multiplayer menu yet. From a terminal you can start the game with --connect=HOST[:PORT] (port 27015 by default) and --name=NAME to join a running voxelgame-server. You see other players, can build together and chat, and the server's world is never written into your own saves. voxelgame --connect=HOST[:PORT] --name=NAME: Join a server instead of opening a local world (command line)","href":"features.html#f-mp-join-server","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Run a dedicated server (voxelgame-server)","text":"The dedicated server is a separate program that the installers do not build. Built from source, `voxelgame-server --world DIR` hosts a world on 127.0.0.1:27015 by default; for LAN play bind your LAN address, for example --bind 192.168.1.20:27015. Its console accepts /list, /say, /save, /stop and /help. /list, /say \u003ctext>, /save, /stop, /help: List players, broadcast, save now, save and stop, show commands (server console)","href":"features.html#f-mp-dedicated-server","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Multiplayer chat","text":"While connected to a server, press Enter to type a chat line and Enter again to send it, or Esc to cancel. Messages and join/leave notices stay on screen for 10 seconds; while typing, your keys do not move the player. Enter: Open the chat line / send it (connected to a server) Esc: Cancel the line being typed (chat line open)","href":"features.html#f-mp-chat","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Multiplayer menu, Host world / Open to LAN and LAN discovery","text":"Planned: an in-game Multiplayer menu with saved servers, direct connect, LAN discovery and hosting your own world for friends. None of these exist yet.","href":"features.html#f-mp-menu-lan-hosting","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Server administration (server.toml, kick/ban/whitelist, password, TUI console)","text":"Planned for a later milestone: a server.toml file, /kick /ban /op /whitelist /time /weather /tp, passwords and a full-screen server console. The current server only has command-line options and five console commands.","href":"features.html#f-mp-server-admin","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Headless screenshots and benchmarks (command line)","text":"Advanced: `voxelgame --headless --screenshot=FILE.png` or `--headless --bench=SECONDS` renders offscreen with the normal renderer, without a window, audio or save writes, and can run beside the open game. Benchmarks print one BENCH line with mean, median, 1%-low and worst frame times. --headless --screenshot=PATH [--screenshot-after=SECS] /…: Capture one PNG or measure frame times, then exit (command line)","href":"features.html#f-headless-screenshots-bench","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"CPU and GPU profiling reports (devtools build)","text":"Developers can build with `cargo build --release --features devtools` and set VOXELGAME_PROFILE_JSON=/path/report.json on a headless --bench run to collect CPU scope and GPU pass timings after the warm-up.","href":"features.html#f-devtools-profiling","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Disposable debug sessions and replays (devtools build)","text":"With a devtools build, `voxelgame --debug-stdio` and `python3 tools/game_debug.py replay \u003cscenario.json>` drive a paused, disposable copy of the game for bug reproduction and screenshots. These sessions never save worlds, play audio or listen on a network port.","href":"features.html#f-debug-sessions","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"App and launcher icon","text":"The Linux menu entry and the macOS app use the Voxelgame cube icon. The Windows Start menu shortcut sets no Voxelgame icon, and the game window itself sets no icon.","href":"features.html#f-app-icon","kind":"Feature"},{"title":"Split alder boards","text":"4× Alder boards; needs 1× Alder log.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Split birch boards","text":"4× Birch boards; needs 1× Birch log.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Split pine boards","text":"4× Pine boards; needs 1× Pine log.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Split hardwood boards","text":"4× Alder boards; needs 1× Hardwood or deadwood (Oak, Fruit tree, Acacia, Willow, Mangrove, Jungle, Cherry, Giant old-growth, Palm, Mesquite, Fig or Cypress log, or Deadwood).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Burn a charcoal pile","text":"2× Charcoal; needs 4× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Cook raw meat","text":"1× Cooked meat; needs 1× Raw meat, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Roast venison","text":"2× Cooked meat; needs 1× Venison, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Blow glass flasks","text":"2× Empty flask; needs 8× Glass, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Fire glass","text":"16× Glass; needs 24× Pale sand, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Kiln-fire gray brick","text":"8× Gray brick; needs 8× River clay, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Glaze roof tile","text":"8× Glazed roof tile; needs 8× River clay, 8× Pale sand, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Burn lime plaster","text":"8× White plaster; needs 8× Limestone, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Dress carved stone","text":"8× Carved stone; needs 8× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Cut sandstone","text":"8× Sandstone; needs 16× Pale sand.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Lacquer timber","text":"8× Lacquered timber; needs 8× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 4× River clay, 1× Charcoal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Weave paper panel","text":"8× Paper lattice panel; needs 8× Reeds, 8× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Assemble red lantern","text":"4× Red lantern; needs 4× Paper lattice panel, 2× Lumen crystal, 2× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Weave reed thatch","text":"8× Reed thatch; needs 8× Reeds, 2× Plant fiber or Wool (mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Dress chalk bricks","text":"8× Chalk bricks; needs 8× Chalk.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Dress ironstone bricks","text":"8× Red ironstone bricks; needs 8× Red ironstone.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Dress basalt bricks","text":"8× Basalt bricks; needs 8× Basalt.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Polish granite","text":"8× Polished granite; needs 8× Granite.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Cover cobble with moss","text":"8× Mossy cobble; needs 8× Natural cobble, 1× Moss.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Stone sword","text":"1× Stone sword; needs 4× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 2× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Stone pickaxe","text":"1× Stone pickaxe; needs 6× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 4× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Stone hatchet","text":"1× Stone hatchet; needs 4× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 4× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Stone shovel","text":"1× Stone shovel; needs 2× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 4× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Alder bow","text":"1× Alder bow; needs 8× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 2× Plant fiber or Wool (mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Reed-bound bow","text":"1× Alder bow; needs 8× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 4× Reeds.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Four arrows","text":"4× Arrow; needs 1× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 2× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 1× Feather.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Four reed arrows","text":"4× Arrow; needs 1× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 2× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 4× Reeds.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Four throwing rocks","text":"4× Throwing rock; needs 4× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Alder rifle","text":"1× Alder rifle; needs 40× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 16× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 8× Lumen crystal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Four rifle rounds","text":"4× Rifle round; needs 8× Natural stone (any of 26 natural stones; mixable), 8× Pale sand, 8× Lumen crystal.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"TNT charge","text":"8× TNT charge; needs 32× Pale sand, 8× Lumen crystal, 8× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable).","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Two dynamite sticks","text":"2× Dynamite; needs 4× TNT charge, 2× Paper lattice panel.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Small sailing boat","text":"1× Small sailing boat; needs 32× Boards of any species (Alder, Birch or Pine boards; mixable), 12× Plant fiber or Wool (mixable), 8× Reeds.","href":"guide-crafting.html#recipes","kind":"Recipe"},{"title":"Open ocean","text":"Deep water beyond the continental shelf, with sparse surface life.","href":"atlas.html#biome=OpenOcean","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Ocean floor","text":"Submerged gravel and clay with scattered kelp colonies and seagrass at their margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=OceanFloor","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Deep kelp forest","text":"Tall underwater kelp colonies rooted on the seabed, with lower seagrass fringes and open-water gaps.","href":"atlas.html#biome=DeepKelpForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Boreal burn","text":"Charred fallen logs among pine, birch and spruce, with bright fireweed colonies and grassy gaps.","href":"atlas.html#biome=BorealBurn","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Snowy tundra","text":"Open tundra where snow lingers in colder sheltered patches and thins to frozen turf, with sparse low plants and lichen.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SnowyTundra","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Shell beach","text":"Pale coast of shell sand and driftwood.","href":"atlas.html#biome=ShellBeach","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Tropical dry forest","text":"Shorter acacia, fig and eucalyptus groves alternate with dry grassy glades, leaf litter and brushy edges.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TropicalDryForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Steppe","text":"Dry grass and gravel plains with low grass colonies, scrub margins and wide open gaps.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Steppe","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Monsoon forest","text":"Jungle, fig and palm stands over muddy ground open into grassy glades, with trunk-climbing vines and fern-rich margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MonsoonForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Gravel shore","text":"Gravel and cobble coast with stranded timber along real shorelines.","href":"atlas.html#biome=GravelShore","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Dune sea","text":"Broad dunes with asymmetric crests, sandstone fragments and extensive open sand.","href":"atlas.html#biome=DuneSea","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Heathland","text":"Purple heather colonies separated by short grass and open rock.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Heathland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Bamboo thicket","text":"Dense jointed bamboo with tender shoots.","href":"atlas.html#biome=BambooThicket","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Dense forest","text":"Closed oak stands alternate with open glades and brushy edges; moss, ferns, leaf litter and fungi follow the canopy.","href":"atlas.html#biome=DenseForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Pine highlands","text":"Upland pines and spruces form uneven stands around open glades, over podzol, ferns, lichen and scattered stone groups.","href":"atlas.html#biome=PineHighlands","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"African thornveld","text":"Scattered acacia above tight thorn-bush colonies and dry-scrub margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AfricanThornveld","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Seagrass lagoon","text":"Shallow sandy lagoon with dense seagrass beds and open sand gaps.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SeagrassLagoon","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Coastal dunes","text":"Sheltered dune-grass tufts on pale coastal sand, with timber along real strandlines.","href":"atlas.html#biome=CoastalDunes","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Desert","text":"Pale dune desert with broad young barrel cacti, slender juveniles and mature plants bearing unequal arms and tapered crowns.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Desert","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Polar desert","text":"Extremely dry, wind-scoured polar gravel and frozen ground.","href":"atlas.html#biome=PolarDesert","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Salt flats","text":"Open salt pans with crust seams and subtle surface patterns; shrubs and stone groups remain absent.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SaltFlats","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Savanna","text":"Open acacia canopies of varied heights above broad tall-grass colonies and dry scrub.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Savanna","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Mediterranean woodland","text":"Compact oak, mesquite and fig among dry-shrub colonies, flowered margins and rocky clearings.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MediterraneanWoodland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Coral shallows","text":"Shallow reef with color-coherent coral colonies, varying heights and seagrass margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=CoralShallows","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Frozen fjord","text":"Snowy glacial inlet above dark deep water and slate scree.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FrozenFjord","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Miombo woodland","text":"Warm acacia, mesquite and fig groves alternate with broad grassy openings and dry, brushy margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MiomboWoodland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Aurora tundra","text":"Open permafrost with faintly luminous lichen; lingering snow breaks into frozen-turf patches along warmer margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AuroraTundra","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Autumn forest","text":"Amber and russet groves alternate with grassy glades; litter, fallen timber and fungi collect beneath the canopy.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AutumnForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Tallgrass prairie","text":"Dense, broad tall-grass colonies over rich soil, with wildflower margins and few trees.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TallgrassPrairie","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Rainforest jungle","text":"Jungle, fig and palm stands alternate with brushy gaps; shaded ferns and bromeliads follow the canopy, while vines cling to real trunks.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Rainforest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Meadow","text":"Open turf with grouped alder, oak and fruit trees, brushy edges and grassy glades that follow the same neighborhood pattern.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Meadow","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Birch grove","text":"Light birch-dominated groves of mixed heights, separated by grassy glades and brushy margins with litter and flowers.","href":"atlas.html#biome=BirchGrove","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Painted hills","text":"Eroded hills of ochre, umber, and sienna clay.","href":"atlas.html#biome=PaintedHills","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Badlands mesa","text":"Eroded mesa country with visible clay strata.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Badlands","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Old-growth forest","text":"Broad veteran crowns rise above younger trees and brushy edges, with open glades, fern-rich shade and fungi beside fallen timber.","href":"atlas.html#biome=OldGrowthForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Temperate rainforest","text":"Moist redwood, alder and giant-tree stands alternate with open glades, mossy roots, ferns, fallen timber and fungi.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TemperateRainforest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Tide pools","text":"Low rocky coast rich in anemones, starfish, and barnacles.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TidePools","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Montane forest","text":"Shorter spruce, pine and birch stands form a belt below alpine ground, with open glades, ferns and lichen margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MontaneForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Pampas grassland","text":"Open grassland with pale plume-grass colonies and low green margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Pampas","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Tropical lowland river","text":"Warm muddy river channels with denser reed fringes and fern-covered banks.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TropicalRiver","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Red desert","text":"Iron-red sand with varied cactus silhouettes, sparse thorny growth and localized red-rock groups separated by open ground.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RedDesert","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Rocky cliffs","text":"Exposed wave-cut boulder coast.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RockyCliffs","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Coastal redwood forest","text":"Tall redwoods stand above younger trees and broadleaf groves, with open glades, fern-rich shade and fungi near fallen timber.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RedwoodForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Black sand beach","text":"Basaltic coast covered by black volcanic sand.","href":"atlas.html#biome=BlackSandBeach","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Australian bushland","text":"Open eucalyptus woodland with dry-shrub colonies and low grassy margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AustralianBushland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Cold gravel river","text":"Cold clear water over gravel, with sparse reed-and-sedge margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=ColdRiver","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Chaparral","text":"Dry scrub hills and shallow hollows hold grouped flat-crowned trees, low bushes, heather and sculpted boulder patches.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Chaparral","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Scrubland","text":"Low mesquite trees and loose thorn-bush colonies separated by dry-scrub margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Scrubland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Alpine meadow","text":"Colonies of mountain and endemic flowers on high turf above the main tree line, with lingering snow on colder ordinary ground.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AlpineMeadow","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Red ironstone steppe","text":"Dry continental grassland over rust-red ironstone outcrops.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RedRockSteppe","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Marsh","text":"Willows and alder above muddy shallows, with dense reed colonies and sedge margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Marsh","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Rolling downs","text":"Broad chalky ridges and hollows carry cropped grass, flint, grouped low growth and open grassy clearings.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RollingDowns","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Flower meadow","text":"Rich wildflower colonies and occasional flowering shrubs alternate with short-grass openings; hives occupy the richer patches.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FlowerMeadow","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Orchard grove","text":"Fruit-tree groves of varied ages alternate with grassy clearings; fallen fruit stays close to actual fruit trees.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Orchard","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Cherry grove","text":"Cherry woodland with petal carpets concentrated around trees.","href":"atlas.html#biome=CherryGrove","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Lavender fields","text":"Broad lavender colonies across rich rolling turf, separated by low grassy margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=LavenderFields","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Wetland fen","text":"Sedge-dominated peat wetland, with cotton grass at the colony margins and open water between stands.","href":"atlas.html#biome=WetlandFen","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Sea stacks","text":"Layered sandstone coast with pale guano patches and low, weathered rock groups.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SeaStacks","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Flooded grassland","text":"Seasonally flooded mud plain with tall reeds and lilies.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FloodedGrassland","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Coral atoll","text":"Low coral cay with shell sand, palms, sparse pale limestone stones on dry ground and shallow reef water.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Atoll","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Floodplain forest","text":"Willow-dominated river woodland with alder and cypress, fern patches and reed-fringed wet ground.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FloodplainForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Rocky highlands","text":"Exposed gravel and cobble uplands with shouldered boulders, scattered conifers and colonies of mountain flowers and lichen.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RockyHighlands","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Blackwater bayou","text":"Tall cypress and willow over dark water, with knees beside cypress roots and lilies in shallow pools.","href":"atlas.html#biome=BlackwaterBayou","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Inland delta","text":"Landlocked mud-and-shallow-water mosaic with broad colonies of tall reeds and sedge margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=InlandDelta","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Taiga bog","text":"Small spruce and pine stands over snowy peat, with cotton-grass colonies and lichen margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TaigaBog","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Chalk heath","text":"Open chalk country carrying purple heath and scattered flint.","href":"atlas.html#biome=ChalkHeath","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Peat bog","text":"Cotton-grass-dominated colonies over saturated peat and bog iron, with sedge at their margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=PeatBog","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Meteor field","text":"Scorched impact country with fused glass and iron fragments.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MeteorField","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Muskeg","text":"Sparse, stunted spruce and pine over waterlogged peat, with sedge colonies and cotton-grass margins.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Muskeg","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Amethyst gorge","text":"Dark gorge walls carry crystal clusters with unequal violet towers rising from low, connected beds of host rock.","href":"atlas.html#biome=AmethystGorge","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Volcanic field","text":"Basaltic ash and lava rock with obsidian, magma and sculpted fumarole-like mineral formations.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Volcanic","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Granite domes","text":"Exfoliating granite uplands with overlapping stone plates, sparse pine stands and open rocky glades.","href":"atlas.html#biome=GraniteDomes","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Hot springs","text":"Descending mineral terrace fans hold shallow pools beside dry sinter bridges and low, uneven mineral cups with recessed mouths.","href":"atlas.html#biome=HotSprings","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"River delta","text":"Low clay-and-gravel river mouths with reed beds grading into sedge at their edges.","href":"atlas.html#biome=RiverDelta","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Cloud forest","text":"Shortened fig-dominated groves and brushy edges occupy humid highlands, with moss near roots and ferns fringed by bromeliads.","href":"atlas.html#biome=CloudForest","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Geyser basin","text":"Geyserite terraces and sulfur-crusted pools dotted with sculpted mineral vents. The vents are static formations, without simulated eruptions.","href":"atlas.html#biome=GeyserBasin","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Limestone pavement","text":"Exposed jointed limestone with thin turf-filled fissures.","href":"atlas.html#biome=LimestonePavement","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Slate quarry hills","text":"Layered slate hills carry angular scree fans, raised fragments and short stepped outcrops.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SlateQuarryHills","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Oasis","text":"Mixed-height palms around fresh water, with dates near their trunks and grass grading into reeds.","href":"atlas.html#biome=Oasis","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Karst pinnacles","text":"Jagged limestone towers draped in hanging vines.","href":"atlas.html#biome=KarstPinnacles","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Snowy peaks","text":"Cold alpine heights with lingering snow, warmer patches of native rock and exposed cliffs beneath the summit.","href":"atlas.html#biome=SnowyPeaks","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Glacial moraine","text":"Unsorted boulders, gravel, and till left at a glacier margin.","href":"atlas.html#biome=GlacialMoraine","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Temperate salt marsh","text":"Sedge and reed colonies around tidal shallows, with open deeper channels.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TemperateSaltMarsh","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Tepui plateau","text":"Rain-darkened high plateau carrying bromeliads and endemic flowers.","href":"atlas.html#biome=TepuiPlateau","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Firn field","text":"High compacted-snow field split by blue crevasses.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FirnField","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Surface lava tubes","text":"Ropy cooled lava above shallow basalt tubes.","href":"atlas.html#biome=LavaTubes","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Ice spikes","text":"Cold field of tall wind-carved ice spires.","href":"atlas.html#biome=IceSpikes","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Glacial outwash plain","text":"Flat sand-and-gravel plain deposited by glacial meltwater.","href":"atlas.html#biome=GlacialOutwash","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Mangrove coast","text":"Tidal mud coast held by mangrove roots.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MangroveCoast","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Mossy highlands","text":"Short pine and spruce groves rise from mossy cobble, with brushy edges, open glades, ferns and lichen.","href":"atlas.html#biome=MossyHighlands","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Frozen lake","text":"Cold lowland water sealed by clear and black ice.","href":"atlas.html#biome=FrozenLake","kind":"Biome atlas"},{"title":"Volcanic caldera","text":"Broad basaltic crater rim surrounding an ash-covered basin.","href":"atlas.html#biome=VolcanicCaldera","kind":"Biome atlas"}];
